WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.840
We are going to try and do an experiment today.
It is called

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.549
the ‘gallium beating heart’. So it is
quite a cool experiment,

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:14.969
you take some molten gallium and then you
submerge it or

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:18.840
you put it underneath a layer of sulphuric
acid. The sulphuric

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.741
acid reacts with the gallium to form gallium
sulphate and it

00:00:22.741 --> 00:00:27.190
changes the surface tension, so it all pulls
up into a ball and

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:31.940
then relaxes and there is this really neat
oscillating reaction

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.840
where you get sulphate on the surface or no
sulphate on the

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.989
surface. So you see big changes in surface
tension and the

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:41.999
piece of gallium is seen to beat like a heart
so we thought we

00:00:41.999 --> 00:00:44.090
would try that today with a bit of gallium
which I am trying to

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:45.309
melt in my hand right now.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:49.479
Many years ago, a professor bet me a hundred
pounds, when

00:00:49.479 --> 00:00:52.659
a hundred pounds was a lot of money, that
the melting point

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:55.949
of gallium was lower than that of caesium
and I said it was

00:00:55.949 --> 00:00:59.760
the other way round, but I was not courageous
enough to

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.519
take up the bet or now I would be rich.

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:06.570
So this is a small nugget of gallium and as
you can see if I

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.060
tap it on the hot plate it is real metal so
now we are going to

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.189
try and melt it, hopefully in my hand.

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:19.010
In my first video about gallium, I said that
it was not a very

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.850
interesting element with not much, many applications
and we

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.189
have had emails from people who were outraged.
There is

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:32.380
one email from a gentleman in America whose
company

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.859
makes tonnes of gallium salts that have quite
important

00:01:35.859 --> 00:01:41.350
applications and of course gallium is particularly
important in

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:48.740
new generation of compounds, so-called semi-conductors,
that are used in the electronics industry.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.429
So if I stand and hold it for long enough,
in theory, the

00:01:52.429 --> 00:01:54.240
temperature should come from my hand, the
heat should

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.840
come from my hand, so it should melt the gallium
and then

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.039
eventually we should have a small amount of
liquid. So

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:04.180
gallium, you can form an amalgam with this
material, with all

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.469
sorts of different materials like indium and
tin. And in fact

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:11.500
indium, tin and gallium is used as the material
inside many,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.010
many thermometers which are used in the medical
industry

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.290
because it is not as toxic as mercury which
was traditionally

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.790
used. You may have heard of Mendeleev. He
was the guy that

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:25.390
sort of conceived or brought together the
periodic table and

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:28.250
at the time that he did this at about 1870,
gallium hadn’t

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.670
been discovered, so he predicted its chemical
and physical

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:35.231
properties and he called it eka-aluminium,
approximately 5

00:02:35.231 --> 00:02:38.960
years after that it was found using a spectroscope.
So now

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.340
we see it is melted, it looks just like a
little ball of mercury

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.240
rolling around on my hand. You can see the
thick skin that

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.170
has developed the surface tension causing
it to form such a

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:55.730
beautiful bulb. We are going to take out our
molten gallium

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.760
and we are going to put it under a layer of
dilute sulphuric

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.422
acid, about the same strength as battery acid,
so now we are

00:03:03.422 --> 00:03:06.990
going to make the acid. And to do that we
are going to dilute

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.141
concentrated sulphuric acid with water. Now
it is always

00:03:10.141 --> 00:03:13.540
important to remember that when you dilute
acid you always

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.560
put acid into water because the reaction can
be exothermic

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.200
and we want to dissipate the heat in the water
so that it does

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.100
not squirt up on our faces. The water was
room temperature,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.010
but if I hold the measuring cylinder here
it is actually very

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:33.090
hot, it is maybe 40, 50, 60oC. So now before
we do our

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:36.760
experiment we have got to allow it to cool.
We do that by

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:39.940
simply running cold water outside the tube.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:43.070
So this is the gallium that we melted on our
hand, now let’s

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:47.730
see what happens when we add the sulphuric
acid. Wow, did

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:48.940
you see that?

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.340
I did. What happened?

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.540
So the shape of the ball or the lump of gallium
has changed,

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.460
because we’ve changed it surface properties,
we’ve

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.040
made gallium sulphate on the surface. The
surface tension

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.090
has got higher and it has pulled up into a
nice ball, ok. So

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.280
that’s the first step of the beating heart.
What we’ve seen is

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.380
the gallium which was relaxed flat on the
surface has pulled

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.240
up really quite sharply and that is the first
stage, or the in-

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.290
beat of the heart, now we’ve got to make
the heart relax. So

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.030
what we are going to do is use dichromate.
Dichromate is a

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.070
fantastic oxidant and we are going to put
a small amount of it

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.070
in and see what happens.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:34.530
So gallium, I realise now is named after France.
It was

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:41.630
discovered by a Frenchman and its name comes
from the old

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:50.460
name for France, Gaul.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.540
So what we are doing in this experiment, we
are changing the

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:57.100
surface tension of the gallium, so these are
the forces that

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:03.090
hold the liquid into a ball or allow it to
wet and flatten on the

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:08.790
surface. So when the gallium is naked, ok?
It’s actually quite

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:12.151
flat and it wets the surface. But when we
put it under

00:05:12.151 --> 00:05:15.460
sulphuric acid we form gallium sulphate on
the surface, this

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.970
increases the surface tension and then pulls
the gallium so it

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:22.370
is in a nice proud ball. What we then do is
we dribble a small

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.190
amount of dichromate solution in, which removes
the

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.630
sulphate from the surface and allows the gallium
to go flat,

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:32.060
and if we add the required amount of dichromate
to the

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.810
amount of acid then we can get the ball to
flatten and come

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:38.140
back up, flatten and come back up, so it looks
like a beating

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:39.140
heart.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.210
At the moment we are really quite excited
at Nottingham

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:47.250
because Steve Liddle, my periodic videos colleague,
has made

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:53.800
a new compound in which he has got an atom
of uranium to

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:58.400
bond to an atom of gallium and this is exciting
because this is

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.870
the first time that chemists have ever seen
a bond between

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:02.870
these two atoms.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:08.210
It sort of turns a terracottery colour, it
is really a deep orangey

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:11.180
brown, it is a bit like brick dust actually.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.280
Of course they are not naked uranium and naked
gallium but

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.840
each of them have other things bonded to them
but it is the

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.490
first time that anybody has seen a gallium-uranium
bond.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:27.610
It was one of those great moments you know,
being a

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:30.790
chemist is like being on a rollercoaster,
you have some days

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:33.930
when you wonder why am I doing this? And you
have

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.330
fantastic days where you totally remember
why you are doing

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:39.530
this. Because it is to be the first, to be
the first person who

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:40.530
has ever made this particular, you know filling
the gap,

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:44.950
whatever it is. It is a real thrill when you
are the first person

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:49.210
to make a compound that does not exist out
in the galaxy

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:52.970
and space, it has never existed on our planet
before but now

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:56.550
we have made it for the first time. That’s
a really good thing

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:57.550
to do

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:02.020
We are really very excited! A new union has
been formed

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.630
within the periodic table and it has been
done here at

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:04.940
Nottingham.

